# STEP IN AND OUT, AND STL OUT (M22).
#
# The M22 gate. Everything before this milestone stayed inside EP3D: a part
# could be built, measured and saved, and there was no way to hand it to
# anybody. This script is the proof that there is now a way OUT and a way BACK.
#
# Run it:
#   ep3d --script examples/step-round-trip.ep3ds
#
# It writes three files under build/ and reads one of them back.

# --- a part worth exporting --------------------------------------------------
#
# Not a plain block: a shelled box with a hole, so the round trip has curved
# faces and an inner shell to lose. A cube would round-trip through almost
# anything, including a writer that quietly threw the geometry away.

sketch Base
tool rect
click -40 -40
click 40 40
pad Case 50

shell Case 4 top

sketch Bores xy 50
tool point
click -22 0
tool point
click 22 0

hole Case Bores 10

echo --- the part, before it leaves ---
solve
measure

# --- out as STEP -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The FORMAT COMES FROM THE EXTENSION. There is no format argument, because a
# .step written as STL is a file whose name lies about it, and the reader at
# the far end would refuse it for a reason that names neither EP3D nor the
# choice that caused it.
#
# What goes out is AP214 in MILLIMETRES, both written into the file itself --
# not left to the reader to assume. A STEP file that omits its unit is read as
# metres by some readers and millimetres by others, and the part arrives either
# right or a thousand times too big with nothing to say which.

export Case build/case.step

# --- out as STL, twice, at two deflections -----------------------------------
#
# STL is TRIANGLES, so it is not the same kind of file at all: it is an
# approximation, and the deflection is how wrong it is allowed to be. Both
# numbers are said out loud in the log rather than defaulted silently, because
# a mesh written at a tolerance nobody chose is a mesh nobody can reproduce.
#
# The coarse one is smaller. That is the whole trade, in two lines.

export Case build/case-coarse.stl 0.5
export Case build/case-fine.stl 0.02

# --- and back in -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An import stores the PATH, not the geometry. What the document keeps is the
# sentence "the solid in that file", and it is answered again on every rebuild
# -- exactly like a face query, which keeps "the top face" and not face #4.
#
# The cost is honest: if that file is deleted, this feature FAILS, loudly and
# by name. A part that quietly kept working after its source vanished would be
# a part nobody could ever reproduce.

import build/case.step as Ghost

# The same number as before it left. Not "a file appeared" -- a file can appear
# holding a part at a thousand times the size, and only the volume can tell.
echo --- the same part, after the round trip ---
solve
measure

# --- and it is a base like any other -----------------------------------------
#
# An import that could not be built on would be a picture rather than a part.
# This drills the imported solid, which needs its real FACES -- the flat top
# face the bore starts from, and the far wall it stops at. A STEP file carries
# those; the STL written a few lines up threw them away and kept triangles, and
# that is exactly why STL is a way out of EP3D and not a way back in.

sketch Late xy 50
tool point
click 0 25

hole Ghost Late 6

echo --- drilled, on geometry that came from a file ---
solve
measure

save build/step-round-trip.ep3d
